exports[`grepsafe.sh given: [case10] argument validation when: [t2] --help flag then: the --help notes block matches snapshot 1`] = `
"usage: grepsafe.sh --pattern 'regex' [options]

options:
  --pattern REGEX    search pattern (required)
  --path DIR         search directory (default: .); repeat to search
                     several
  --from @stdin      search piped text, not files (implied when
                     text is piped in)
  --glob GLOB        file filter glob (e.g., '*.ts')
  --type TYPE        file type filter (e.g., ts, py, sh)
  --context N        context lines around matches
  --files-only       show file paths only; wins over --count
  --count            show match counts per file
  -i                 case insensitive
  --head N           limit output to N rows; the tally then reads
                     '<files|rows|lines>: <total> (first N)', so a cut
                     is never silent. the label names what it counts:
                     files (--files-only|--count), rows (--context),
                     lines otherwise
  --tail N           keep the last N rows; the tally then reads
                     '<label>: <total> (last N)'. not with --head
  --multiline        let a match span lines
  --output MODE      vibes|pipeable (default: vibes)

notes:
  to name a path opts into every skip that would hide it.
  --path, or a --glob whose leading segments are a real directory,
  lifts --hidden and the ignore rules and follows a named symlink,
  scoped to that root. a tree met deeper on the walk keeps its
  default skips. a glob with no literal prefix ('**/*.md')
  names no path.

  a glob reads as .gitignore reads it, relative to --path: one
  with a slash is anchored ('docs/*.md' skips 'docs/sub/'), one
  with none matches at any depth. each brace alternative names
  its own root: '{src/**,.behavior/**}' walks both.

  a walk skips hidden paths. when a hidden file holds a match, the
  output says so — beside a zero or beside results — and names the
  --path that reaches it.

  piped text is the subject when it is present. piped text beside
  --path, --glob, or --type names two subjects, so it is refused.

  rg (ripgrep) is required. with no rg, grepsafe refuses rather
  than report a silent 0.

  every search root must sit within the repo. a '..' or a leading
  '/' in --glob is refused, same as a --path outside the repo.

  --output pipeable renders an empty result as empty (exit 0), so
  'no file matched the glob' and 'no line matched the pattern'
  read alike. the vibes default names which zero it found.
  --output 'direct' is renamed 'pipeable'; the old value is
  refused (exit 2) with the new name.
  --head and --tail take a positive integer of at most 18 digits;
  any other value is refused (exit 2), never reinterpreted.
  every --path is searched. a bare positional root stands alone:
  beside another root it is refused (exit 2), since an unquoted
  glob the shell expanded lands there too.

example:
  grepsafe.sh --pattern 'paddleOut|wipeout' --glob 'src/**/*.ts'
"
`;

